\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mathematics and programming often describe the same pattern in different languages.
 Mathematics begins with a collection of objects,
 equips them with operations,
 distinguishes particular objects when necessary,
 and states laws relating them.
 A 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},breaklines=true,tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
python
\end_layout

\end_inset

 type begins with values,
 gives them operations through functions and methods,
 may provide distinguished values through its class,
 and specifies how all of these are expected to behave.
 This text develops its constructions by moving deliberately between those two descriptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Reading the correspondence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The correspondence is a reading convention,
 not an identification of mathematics with 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},breaklines=true,tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
python
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 When a mathematical object and a piece of code are placed beside one another,
 the claim is only that they play the same role in the construction at hand.
 Features with no faithful counterpart are stated separately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Functions give the notation its first example.
 A function from a type 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to a type 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is written 
\begin_inset Formula $f\colon A\to B$
\end_inset

;
 this may be read as saying that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 has the function type 
\begin_inset Formula $A\to B$
\end_inset

.
 The same convention applies to every object:
 
\begin_inset Formula $x\colon A$
\end_inset

 says that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 has type 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Classical set notation often expresses the corresponding carrier-element relation as 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in A$
\end_inset

.
 This text uses the colon for typing and reserves 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

 for membership in an actual set or container 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 The former states what 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is;
 the latter asks whether 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 contains it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
carrier A mathematical type 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 corresponds to a 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},breaklines=true,tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
python
\end_layout

\end_inset

 type.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
element A judgement 
\begin_inset Formula $x\colon A$
\end_inset

 corresponds to a value 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 of that type.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
operation A map 
\begin_inset Formula $f\colon A\to B$
\end_inset

 corresponds to a function or method,
 often exposed by an operator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
constant A distinguished object 
\begin_inset Formula $e\colon A$
\end_inset

 corresponds to a value or constructor supplied by the class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
law A mathematical equation or relation corresponds to a behavioural contract.
 The interface can describe its shape;
 a proof establishes the law,
 while tests check an implementation against it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The corresponding annotations in 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},breaklines=true,tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
python
\end_layout

\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},breaklines=true,tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
x:
 A
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},breaklines=true,tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
f:
 typing.Callable[[A],
 B]
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 They describe which values a callable accepts and returns;
 they do not claim that the callable is pure,
 total,
 or otherwise identical to a mathematical function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The spelling of an operation does not determine its meaning.
 The operator 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},breaklines=true,tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
|
\end_layout

\end_inset

 may express disjunction,
 set union,
 or the union of types;
 its carrier determines which reading applies.
 Reusing a symbol is useful precisely when the same structural role appears in several settings.
 Whenever such a pairing appears below,
 the surrounding definition supplies the meaning,
 something that is true even in the context of mathematics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The object-oriented reading
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},breaklines=true,tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
class
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is a natural unit for this translation because it places a carrier and its operations under one name.
 A value 
\begin_inset Formula $x\colon A$
\end_inset

 is represented by an instance of the corresponding class,
 its methods implement operations,
 and its class-level interface supplies distinguished values or constructors.
 Type annotations describe which values an operation accepts and returns.
 They express the outline of a structure,
 while its laws remain part of its contract.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Composition and inheritance answer different questions.
 Composition says that a value
\emph on
 has
\emph default
 a representation made from other values:
 a rational number,
 for example,
 has a numerator and a denominator.
 Inheritance says that one type
\emph on
 is
\emph default
 (a specialization of) another and may be used wherever the more general contract is expected.
 In the notation above,
 a value 
\begin_inset Formula $x\colon B$
\end_inset

 may be used where a value of type 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is expected whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 specializes 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 It is therefore appropriate only when the subclass preserves that contract,
 not merely when inheriting its code is convenient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Abstract base classes make this division explicit.
 Abstract methods name the operations a concrete carrier must provide;
 concrete methods may supply operations that follow from them.
 A subclass then chooses a representation and implements the primitive operations,
 while inheriting the common consequences.
 This reduces repetition without hiding which mathematical assumptions the shared behaviour uses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each construction in this text therefore follows the same pattern:
 state the carrier,
 operations,
 distinguished values,
 and laws;
 translate the supported structure into a 
\begin_inset listings
lstparams "language=Python,fontfamily=tt,fontsize={\scriptsize},breaklines=true,tabsize=4"
inline true
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
python
\end_layout

\end_inset

 interface;
 choose its representation;
 and check the resulting correspondence,
 naming any approximation.
 Code is used as a display language for these definitions,
 not as a substitute for them.
 Built-in names are written bare,
 while names from the standard library are qualified by their module.
 With this convention in place,
 the later correspondences can be read without repeatedly explaining the translation itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
From operations to structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 be a type.
 A binary operation on 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is a value 
\begin_inset Formula $\circ\colon S\times S\to S$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently,
 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $a\colon S$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b\colon S$
\end_inset

,
 their combination satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $a\circ b\colon S$
\end_inset

.
 The operation is therefore closed on its carrier.
 A carrier equipped with one closed binary operation is called a 
\emph on
magma
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Closure gives the operation its shape but says nothing about how repeated combinations behave.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(a\circ b)\circ c=a\circ(b\circ c)\qquad(a,b,c\colon S),
\]

\end_inset

 then the operation is associative and the magma is a 
\emph on
semigroup
\emph default
.
 No new operation has been added;
 the same carrier has acquired a stronger law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If there is also a distinguished value 
\begin_inset Formula $e\colon S$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e\circ a=a=a\circ e\qquad(a\colon S),
\]

\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 is an identity and the semigroup is a 
\emph on
monoid
\emph default
.
 The identity may equally be read as a nullary operation:
 it accepts no values and returns one of type 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 if every 
\begin_inset Formula $a\colon S$
\end_inset

 has a value 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{-1}\colon S$
\end_inset

 satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a^{-1}\circ a=e=a\circ a^{-1}\qquad(a\colon S),
\]

\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is the inverse of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and the monoid is a 
\emph on
group
\emph default
.
 Both equations belong to the contract because the operation need not be commutative.
 Inversion is therefore a unary operation on the carrier,
 assigning 
\begin_inset Formula $a\mapsto a^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The complete correspondence can be stated directly as a class hierarchy.
\end_layout

